          "The **parietal pleura** is supplied by **systemic (intercostal) arteries** at high hydrostatic pressure, so fluid **filters from parietal capillaries into the space** by Starling forces, and is removed by **lymphatic stomata of the parietal pleura** (mainly diaphragmatic and mediastinal) that can increase drainage about **20-fold** before fluid accumulates. [Harrison 22e]",
          "An effusion forms by one of five mechanisms: **raised hydrostatic pressure** (heart failure), **low oncotic pressure** (nephrotic syndrome, cirrhosis), **increased capillary permeability** (infection, inflammation, tumour), **blocked lymphatic drainage** (tumour, radiation) or **fluid passing from the peritoneum through diaphragmatic defects** (ascites, peritoneal dialysis). [Harrison 22e]",
